Disable modpost's two build-time KMI gates introduced with the 6.13+ trees.

Contents of scripts/mod/modpost.c changed:

  case 't'  trim_unused_exports = true    ->  removed
      Drops every export that no in-tree module imports, so an export that
      only a stock/vendor module needs disappears from __ksymtab and the
      module fails with "Unknown symbol <sym>" (err -2). Example on 6.18:
      get_fs_type (fs/filesystems.c) and d_alloc_name (fs/dcache.c).

  case 'u'  default_symbol_protected_status = true  ->  removed
      Flags every export that is not named in the -u whitelist as
      KSYM_FLAG_PROTECTED. kernel/module/main.c then refuses imports of
      those symbols from a module whose signature did not verify:
      "<mod>: Cannot use protected symbol <sym>" (err -13, EACCES).

Both are reversed here at the source because neither has a file to delete on
these trees: the protected set is the complement of the KMI list, and the
trim set is derived from module imports.

CONFIG is deliberately untouched:
  - CONFIG_TRIM_UNUSED_KSYMS stays y, so Kleaf's
    "actual 'y', expected ''" assertion is not involved;
  - the -u whitelist is still passed, so handle_white_list_exports() keeps
    marking those symbols used/unprotected;
  - kmi_symbol_list_strict_mode still passes: verify_ksymtab.py only fails
    when a symbol that is *listed* is missing from ksymtab
    (set(kmi_symbols) - set(ksymtab_symbols)), never on extra exports.
  - __kexporttab (-p) is unaffected; the protected_module_names_list strip
    in the same CI step removes it.

Effect on the ABI: no CRC changes. Exports are a superset, so Module.symvers
gains names; the ABI gate joins on name and compares CRCs, so additions do
not register as drift.

--- a/scripts/mod/modpost.c
+++ b/scripts/mod/modpost.c
@@ -2359,11 +2359,9 @@
 			files_source = optarg;
 			break;
 		case 't':
-			trim_unused_exports = true;
 			break;
 		case 'u':
 			unused_exports_white_list = optarg;
-			default_symbol_protected_status = true;
 			break;
 		case 'W':
 			extra_warn = true;
